WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.540
These people are on a pilgrimage.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.300
They're in one of the most remote parts of the United States,

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.320
to see something
spectacular that happens every year.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.280
An icon of the American West.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.900
This is a sage grouse.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.480
And this is its mating dance.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.340
The sage grouse once numbered in the
millions across this entire region.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.220
But today, they are on the verge of endangerment.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.400
And the area they live on has shrunk by half.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.980
That's a problem. And
not just because it's fun to look at.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.600
It's because the sage grouse is actually
really important.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.780
And to understand why, you have to understand its dance.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.460
This is a female sage grouse.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.320
And this is a male sage grouse.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.140
During mating season, it's big and flamboyant looking,

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.300
all so that it can attract mates.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.060
It's sort of like a peacock in that way.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.780
The spiky tail, the puffed out chest —
they don't serve a clear function

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.460
except to be attractive.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.680
These white feathers on
its chest are rough and spiky.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.280
And for the first step of its dance, the
sage-grouse takes a deep breath

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.640
and it swishes its wings against those spiky feathers.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.940
It sounds like this this.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.900
These yellow things are its vocal sac. It's
actually one esophagus

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.020
with a strong muscle in the center.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.680
When the sage grouse takes in a gulp of air, it contracts.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.460
When it breathes out. the vocal
sac pops.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.180
That sounds like this.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.100
Female sage grouse can hear these sounds from up to two miles away.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.220
And then it's a competition.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.260
The strongest male dancers crowd out the weaker ones.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.620
The winner gets to mate with most of the females.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.740
After they mate, the females go up to ten miles away to nest.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.780
But this arena, where the competition goes down,

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.880
that place stays the same every year.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.640
Sage grouse live across this whole vast
area. But year after year,

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.020
they each come back to do the courtship ritual in the same exact spot:

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.020
One of these blue dots.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.360
It's why sage-grouse watchers always
know where to find them.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.120
That mating area is called a lek.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.280
And it's a big part of why sage grouse matter so much.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:49.720
Coming back to the same place every year means that sage grouse are really easy to keep track of.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.160
Some leks have been monitored
by researchers for more than 75 years.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.720
They're so easy to count that
conservationists consider them an indicator species.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.780
That means they use the number of sage grouse as a sort of proxy

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.920
for how healthy the entire
sagebrush ecosystem is.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.480
If sage grouse are doing well, there's a good chance that elk, and pygmy rabbits,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.980
and the 350 plant and animal species in the region are doing okay too.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.880
That also means that if you want to protect wildlife in the American West,

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.700
figuring out how to protect the
sage grouse is a good strategy.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.140
In 2008, Wyoming implemented a new policy aimed
at protecting the bird.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:37.560
It ended up also having major benefits for another animal — the mule deer.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.100
But because sage grouse are so easy to count, we also know that they are in trouble.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:50.680
The lek is where the sage grouse mates, but it's just the epicenter of a much larger range where they live.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.900
And in order for them to keep
coming back to the lek to reproduce,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.580
sage grouse need the entire range to be
undisturbed.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.680
That means if human activity alters this
range, they tend to not return to the lek —

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.540
they don't reproduce.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:10.240
Montana started keeping track of sage grouse leks in 2002.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.580
Since then, their population in the
state has fallen by nearly half.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.620
There are a lot of reasons for this
decline — invasive plant species,

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.060
wildfires,

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.720
but a big one is drilling and mining.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.420
"Western states have increased production dramatically in recent years."

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.320
"A huge amount of new oil."

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:34.340
"Oil, coal, and natural gas dominates the landscape and the economy."

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.400
Wyoming has the most sage grouse of any state.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.200
Here's a map of leks in Wyoming.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.940
And these are oil and gas fields.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:51.920
In 2015, governors from four states announced a plan with the federal government to protect the sage grouse.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.720
It banned mineral mining across ten million acres
of sage grouse habitat.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:04.820
And it restricted oil and gas leasing in 13,000 square miles of the most critical habitat areas.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:09.240
It was hailed as the largest conservation effort in US history.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:16.460
But today, Donald Trump's administration isn't enforcing the restrictions on oil and gas leasing.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.340
And it canceled the ban on mining here.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.760
And the numbers reflect that: since he took office,

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.760
oil and gas leasing on public land in the US has skyrocketed.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.740
And on land the 2015 plan was supposed to protect,

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:34.120
leasing has gone up tenfold.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:39.680
Today, officials predict that sage grouse numbers will keep falling.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.320
The sage grouse is an indicator species.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.660
It means that the sage grouse can tell you a lot

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:52.280
about the health of the entire
sagebrush ecosystem, across the American West.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.260
But they can also indicate
something about American policy.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:00.700
About the power that fossil fuel and mining companies have over government.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.880
About what we choose to protect.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.120
And what we don't.

